   The cell consists of a hydrogen electrode (anode) and a copper electrode (cathode). The reaction is:
   \[
   \text{Zn(s)} + \text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + \text{Cu(s)}
   \]

   The standard cell potential (E°) is the difference between the cathode and anode standard reduction potentials:
   \[
   E^{\circ} = E_{\text{cathode}} - E_{\text{anode}} = 0.34 \text{ V} - 0.0 \text{ V} = 0.34 \text{ V}
   \]

   The Nernst equation for the cell is:
   \[
   E = E^{\circ} - \frac{0.0591}{n} \log \frac{I_{\text{cathode}}}{I_{\text{anode}}}
   \]
   For this cell, the reaction is:
   \[
   \text{H}_2(g) + 2\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{Cu}(s) + \2\text{H}^+(aq)
   \]
   The number of electrons transferred (n) is 2.

