ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS°) - Σ(n * ΔS°)

where n is the number of moles of each substance and ΔS° is the standard entropy of each substance.

The reaction is:

3CO(g) + 2O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the standard entropies (ΔS°) of CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K. From standard thermodynamic tables, we find:

ΔS°(CO) = 227.3 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 238.1 J/mol·K

Now we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = (3 * ΔS°(CO2)) - (3 * ΔS°(CO) + 2 * ΔS°(O2))
