What is the dimensional representation of the coefficient of friction?  
a. \( [ML^2T^{-2}] \)  
b. \( [MLT^{-2}] \)  
c. \( [M^0L^0T^0] \)  
d. \( [ML^{-1}] \)
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The coefficient of friction (\(\mu\)) is a dimensionless quantity, meaning it does not have any physical units like mass, length, or time. It is a ratio of forces, and the units cancel out.  

\[
\mu = \frac{\text{Force of friction}}{\text{Normal force}} = \frac{F}{\text{N}}
\]  
Since both \( F \) and \( N \) are in the same units (e.g., Newtons, which are \([MLT^{-2}] \)), the units cancel out:  
\[
\left[\frac{[MLT^{-2}]}{\text{M}[LT^{-2}]}\right] = [M^0 L^0 T^0]
\]  
Thus, the dimensional representation of the coefficient of friction is \([M^0 L^0 T^0]\).  

The correct answer is \(\boxed{c}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

