For a non-recoverable system, the reliability function \( R_S(t) \) is the fraction of systems that survive \( t \) years.  

### a) Series System  
In a series system, the system fails if all components fail. The probability of failure is the product of the failure probabilities of each component.  

Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability function of the \( i \)-th component, and \( F_i(t) \) be its failure function. For a series system with \( n \) components, the system reliability is:  
\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times \cdots \times R_n(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i(t)
\]  
This can also be written as:  
\[
R_S(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - F_i(t))
\]  

### b) Parallel System  
In a parallel system, the system succeeds if all but may only only one component survives. The system fails if all components fail. The reliability is the product of the failure probabilities of each component.  

For a parallel system with \( n \) components, the system reliability is:  
\[
R_S(t) = 1 - F_S(t) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} F_i(t)
\]  
This means the system succeeds if all components survive, so the system's reliability is the complement of the probability that all components fail.  

### c) Combination of Series and Parallel Structures  
For a combination of series and parallel structures, the reliability is calculated by combining the results of the individual series and parallel components.  

