\[
\boxed{y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)}
\]  
This equation is derived from the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \), which is the derivative \( f'(a) \), and the point \( (a, f(a)) \), which lies on the line. The slope of the line is \( f'(a) \), and the slope of a line with slope \( m \) and point \( (x_1, y_1) \) is given by the equation:  
\[
y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)
\]  
Substituting \( m = f'(a) \), \( x_1 = a \), and \( y_1 = f(a) \), we get:  
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
Rearranging the equation, we obtain the standard form:  
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]  
This equation represents the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).  

