","(1) Simplify the expression $\dfrac{\cos (180 ^{\circ} + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360 ^{\circ)}}{\sin (-\alpha - 180 ^{\circ}) \cdot \cos (-180^{\circ} - \alpha)}$.
1. $\cos (180^{\circ} + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$
2. $\sin (\alpha + 360^{\circ}) = \sin \alpha$
3. $\sin (-\alpha - 180^{\circ}) = \sin (-\alpha - 180^{\circ}) = -\sin (\alpha + 180^{\circ}) = -(-\sin \alpha) = \sin \alpha$
4. $\cos (-180^{\circ} - \alpha) = \cos (180^{\circ} + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$

Thus, the expression becomes:
\[
