\[
\int_{-1}^{1} 2^{x} d x = \left[ \frac{1}{\ln 2} 2^x \right]_{-1}^{1} = \frac{1}{\ln 2} \left( 2^1 - 2^{-1} \right) = \frac{1}{\ln 2} \left( 2 - \frac{1}{2} \right) = \frac{3}{2 \ln 2}.
\]
(b) To evaluate the integral $\int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x$, we use the linearity of the integral and the known antiderentials:
\[
\int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x = 3 \int_{0}^{1} e^{x} d x + 2 \int_{0}^{1} \sqrt{x} d x = 3 \left[ e^{x} \right]_{0}^{1} + 2 \left[ \frac{2}{3} x^{3/2} \right]_{0}^{1}.
\]
