<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To verify the unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix, we start with the definition of the \(S\)-matrix:  
\[
S_{mn} = \langle n \rangle \langle m \rangle,
\]  
where \(|n\rangle\) and \(|m\rangle\) are the states of the \(S\)-matrix.  

The unitarity condition is:  
\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{nl} = \delta_{mn},
\]  
where \(S_{lm}^*\) is the complex conjugate of \(S_{lm)\) and \(\delta_{mn}\) is the Kronecker delta.  

Substituting the definition of the \(S\)-matrix:  
\[
\sum_l \langle n | n \rangle \langle m | m \rangle^* = \delta_{mn}.
\]  

Since the \(S\)-matrix is unitary, it preserves the norm of the states, so \(|n\rangle \langle n| = 1\) and \(|m\rangle \langle m| = 1\). Thus, the \(S\)-matrix satisfies:  
\[
||n\rangle\rangle = \sum_l S_{lm} |l\rangle^* = 1,
\]  
and similarly for \(|m\rangle\).  

Substituting this into the unitarity condition:  
\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{nl} = \sum_l \langle n | n \rangle \langle m | m \rangle^* = \delta_{mn}.
\]  

Thus, the unitarity condition is satisfied.  

\boxed{\text{The unitarity condition is verified.}}
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

