","To determine the range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy, we need to consider the uncertainty due to the finite lifetime of the particle. The lifetime of a particle is inversely proportional to its natural width, which is related to the energy resolution of the measurement.

The energy resolution \(Q\) is related to the natural width \(\Gamma\) of the particle by the formula:

\[
Q \geq \hbar \Gamma
\]

Given the lifetime \(T \approx 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\text{ s}\), the natural width is:

\[
\Gamma = \frac{1}{2T} = \frac{1}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 2.5 \times 10^{22} \text{ s}^{-1}
\]

The energy resolution \(Q\) is:

\[
Q \geq \hbar \Gamma = \frac{h}{2\pi \Gamma} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}}{2\pi \times 2.5 \times 10^{22} \text{ s}^{-1}} = 6.7 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}
\]

Convert the energy resolution from Joules to MeV:

\[
Q \approx 6.7 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} \approx 4.2 \text{ MeV}
\]

The total resolution \(Q_{\text{total}}\) is typically the sum of the intrinsic resolution and the radiative correction. The radiative correction is typically 1.2 times the natural width, so:

\[
Q_{\text{total}} \approx 4.2 \text{ MeV}
\]

The range of outcomes for the measurement of the Σ⁺ rest energy is:

\[
\Delta E \approx \pm Q_{\text{total}} = \pm 4.2 \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the expected range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is:

\[
\boxed{1385 \pm 4.2 \text{ MeV}}
\]

